## 7. COMPLETE MULTILINGUAL SUPPORT

Implement full multilingual support across the entire application. Language support must be part of the underlying architecture, not an optional cosmetic feature.

### Required languages

Support all six languages:

1. English — `en`
2. Tamil — `ta`
3. Telugu — `te`
4. Hindi — `hi`
5. Malayalam — `ml`
6. Kannada — `kn`

English is the default language on the first launch unless the existing application specifies another default.

Provide a language selector that allows users to switch languages at any time.

Display each language using its native name:

- English
- தமிழ்
- తెలుగు
- हिन्दी
- മലയാളം
- ಕನ್ನಡ

### 7.1 Language selector

Place the language selector somewhere easy to discover, such as the settings screen, profile menu, or application header.

The selector must:

- Show the currently selected language.
- Present all six supported languages.
- Change the entire supported interface immediately after selection.
- Update labels, navigation, forms, buttons, dialogs, validation messages, notifications, and other localized content.
- Persist the selection across app restarts.
- Work consistently across all screens.
- Avoid requiring the user to restart the application unless the platform genuinely requires it.

If a saved language is unavailable or invalid, safely fall back to English.

### 7.2 Centralized translation architecture

Never hardcode user-facing text throughout the UI components.

Create a centralized localization system using the platform's recommended internationalization mechanism.

For web applications, use an appropriate internationalization library and translation resource files.

For native Android applications, use Android resource localization, including `strings.xml` and language-specific resource directories, while maintaining compatibility with the chosen UI framework.

Organize translations consistently. Example conceptual structure:

- `locales/en`
- `locales/ta`
- `locales/te`
- `locales/hi`
- `locales/ml`
- `locales/kn`

Use the exact directory conventions recommended by the chosen framework.

Each language must contain a complete, consistent set of translation keys.

Example conceptual keys:

- `app.title`
- `navigation.home`
- `navigation.settings`
- `common.save`
- `common.cancel`
- `common.loading`
- `common.retry`
- `form.required`
- `form.invalidInput`
- `errors.network`
- `language.select`

Use the appropriate localization framework's syntax in the actual implementation.

Do not implement translations using scattered conditional statements such as `if language == Tamil` throughout the UI.

### 7.3 Translate the entire interface

Audit every screen and translate all user-visible text, including:

- Application title and branding text where localization is appropriate.
- Onboarding screens.
- Navigation labels.
- Dashboard headings.
- Section titles and descriptions.
- Buttons and links.
- Forms, placeholders, and field labels.
- Dropdown options.
- Search hints.
- Tooltips.
- Dialogs and confirmation messages.
- Validation errors.
- Authentication messages.
- Loading indicators and empty states.
- Success and failure messages.
- Notifications and alerts.
- Settings screens.
- Reports and summaries.
- Accessibility labels and descriptions.
- Help text and instructions.
- Generated content where applicable.

Do not leave English-only labels in the Tamil, Telugu, Hindi, Malayalam, or Kannada interface unless the text is an intentionally untranslated brand name, technical identifier, or user-provided content.

Do not translate programming identifiers, database keys, API fields, or internal enum values. Localize their user-facing representations instead.

### 7.4 Translation quality

Use natural, readable translations appropriate for the intended users.

Do not blindly translate every English phrase word by word. Preserve the original meaning, tone, and purpose.

For technical terms:

- Retain standard technical terms when that is the clearest choice.
- Add a simple localized explanation when helpful.
- Use consistent terminology across all screens.
- Avoid inconsistent mixtures of scripts and languages.
- Preserve proper nouns and brand names where appropriate.

Use the same translation key for the same concept across the application.

Do not display raw translation keys, missing-key identifiers, or programming placeholders to the user.

### 7.5 Indian-language typography and layout

Ensure the interface correctly renders Tamil, Telugu, Devanagari, Malayalam, and Kannada scripts.

- Use fonts that support the required scripts.
- Use appropriate line heights and text sizes.
- Prevent clipping of vowel marks, combining marks, and complex glyphs.
- Allow labels and buttons to expand for longer translations.
- Avoid fixed-width controls that truncate localized text.
- Test line wrapping on small screens.
- Ensure screen readers can interpret localized content.
- Verify that font fallback works when the preferred font is unavailable.

Use native platform fonts where they provide reliable script coverage. Include additional licensed fonts only when necessary.

### 7.6 Number, date, time, and currency formatting

Use locale-aware formatting for:

- Dates.
- Times.
- Numbers.
- Decimal separators.
- Percentages.
- Currency.
- Units of measurement.

Use Indian rupees (`₹`, INR) when the application's financial context requires Indian currency. Do not blindly replace a different explicitly required currency.

Avoid hardcoding date formats such as `MM/DD/YYYY` throughout the application.

Store dates and numeric values in suitable machine-readable formats and localize their presentation.

### 7.7 Dynamic and AI-generated content

If the application generates AI responses, explanations, recommendations, reports, summaries, or conversational responses, the selected language must also control that generated content wherever the feature supports it.

Pass the selected language explicitly to the AI service.

Use a language instruction equivalent to:

"Respond in the user's selected language: [SELECTED_LANGUAGE]. Use natural, clear, culturally appropriate language. Preserve technical accuracy and all required information. Do not switch to English unnecessarily. Retain standard technical terms when appropriate and explain them in the selected language when useful."

Do not assume that translating the UI automatically translates AI responses.

If the model returns content in the wrong language:

- Validate the response when feasible.
- Retry or request correction when appropriate.
- Handle the failure gracefully.
- Do not falsely guarantee perfect language detection or translation.

Keep structured response fields stable internally while localizing their visible labels.

For example, a report can internally store a stable value such as `high_risk`, while displaying the appropriate translated label to the user.

### 7.8 Speech and voice functionality

If the application includes voice input, speech recognition, audio playback, or text-to-speech:

- Use the selected language where supported.
- Configure the appropriate language code for the speech service.
- Handle unavailable language packs and unsupported locales.
- Show a useful message if the selected speech language cannot be used.
- Provide a text-based alternative wherever practical.
- Do not silently substitute an unrelated language.
- Keep speech language settings consistent with the selected application language unless the user can explicitly configure them separately.

Do not add voice functionality if it is outside the application's requirements.

### 7.9 Localization persistence and consistency

Save the selected language using the platform's appropriate persistent preference mechanism.

On launch:

1. Read the saved language.
2. Validate that it is supported.
3. Load the corresponding translations.
4. Initialize the UI in that language.
5. Apply the language consistently to new screens and background-generated user-facing messages.

Ensure language changes do not unintentionally clear form data, sign the user out, delete records, reset navigation, or discard completed work.

Keep internal database records and API contracts stable when the display language changes.

### 7.10 Translation completeness checks

Create a translation key audit or equivalent test.

Verify that:

- Every required key exists in all six languages.
- No language contains accidentally missing translations.
- No raw key names appear in the UI.
- Placeholder variables are preserved correctly.
- Plural forms are handled properly where relevant.
- Dynamic values are inserted safely.
- Long text does not break the layout.
- All supported languages render correctly on target devices.

Do not mark multilingual support as complete if only the home screen or language selector is translated.

