## 11. IMPLEMENTATION WORKFLOW

Follow this sequence internally while producing one coherent final application.

### Phase 1 — Inspect and plan

- Read the complete project brief.
- Inspect the existing codebase when supplied.
- Identify required features and existing constraints.
- Choose the correct architecture and dependencies.
- Define the data models and main user workflows.
- Establish the localization strategy before creating UI text.

### Phase 2 — Build the foundation

- Configure the project.
- Establish the design system.
- Set up routing and navigation.
- Configure application state.
- Create data models and persistence.
- Configure API integrations.
- Create the translation resources for all six languages.

### Phase 3 — Implement all features

- Build every required screen.
- Implement all business logic.
- Connect UI components to real state.
- Connect the data layer.
- Implement actual API and AI integrations where required.
- Add form validation and error handling.
- Implement complete localization.
- Implement all required offline and synchronization behavior.

### Phase 4 — Integrate

- Connect every screen to the appropriate services.
- Verify that the complete user journey works.
- Remove duplicate or conflicting implementations.
- Ensure state and navigation remain consistent.
- Verify that switching languages affects every applicable screen.

### Phase 5 — Test and repair

- Run build and test commands.
- Fix compilation and runtime errors.
- Exercise the main user workflows.
- Test each supported language.
- Check responsive behavior and accessibility.
- Recheck the project after fixes.

### Phase 6 — Finalize

- Ensure the application starts successfully.
- Verify that all required files are present.
- Provide clear setup and launch instructions.
- Identify any external configuration or credentials still needed.
- State which tests were actually executed and their results.
- Document any genuine limitations that could not be resolved.

These phases describe the implementation process. They do not mean that you should stop after producing a plan or complete only the first phase.

**Proceed through the full implementation and testing workflow before declaring the task complete.**

